# Plugin triggers & handlers

## Shape

Export factories that take `app: PluginAppApi` (and optional services) and return an object that `satisfies HandlerDefinitionProps` / `TriggerDefinitionProps`:

```ts
export const createSendMessageHandler = (app: PluginAppApi) => {
	return {
		name: 'Send Message',
		fields: [/* … */],
		execute: (_action, handler, context, next) => {
			// …
			next();
		}
	} satisfies HandlerDefinitionProps;
};
```

## Folder layout

Mirror the definition tree: `handler/<category>/…`, `trigger/<category>/…`. Register under nested `{ name, children }` groups in the plugin `index.ts` (see Twitch/OBS).

## Fields

- Field keys are slugified from `name` (`'As bot'` → `'as-bot'`).
- Read values via the plugin’s `getFieldValue` / `resolveFieldText` helpers — not by scanning raw field arrays.
- Call `configureFieldValueResolver(app)` early in plugin boot when the plugin has handlers/triggers.

## Triggers

- Pair `activate` / `deactivate` with subscription dispose helpers (`setTriggerSubscription` / `disposeTriggerSubscription`).
- Prefer shared helpers when the plugin has them: `createActivate`, `createDeactivate`, `createOnTest`, `evaluateWith`.
- Provide `onTest` with a realistic test context when other triggers in the plugin do.

## Docs

After adding or changing definitions, run `pnpm generate:docs-reference` (see `docs-site-sync.mdc`).
